  "context_pill": "PGBP Assessment & Total Income",
  "example_summary": "This case study involves computing the total income and tax liability for Dinkar Synthetics Ltd. for AY 2026-27, focusing on PGBP adjustments like inventory valuation, gratuity provisions, intangible asset depreciation, and TDS compliance.",
  "source_provision": "### Statutory Summary Matrix\n\n| Area / Provision | Statutory Section / Rule | Operative Threshold / Condition | Tax Treatment / Legal Consequence |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Inventory Valuation** | ICDS 2 & ICDS 9 | Qualifying assets requiring 12+ months for saleable condition | Interest cost excluded from finished goods inventory unless borrowing costs qualify |\n| **Gratuity Provision** | Section 40A(7) | Provision based on actuarial valuation vs actual payment | Provision is disallowed; actual gratuity paid is allowed as a deduction |\n| **Intangible Assets** | Section 32 | Franchise fees paid for obtaining business rights | Eligible for depreciation at 25 percent as an intangible asset |\n| **Theft Loss in Transit** | Section 28 / Section 29 | Loss of cash during transit for business operations | Fully allowable as revenue expenditure being incidental to business |\n| **TDS Non-Deposit** | Section 40(a)(i) | Royalty paid to non-resident without depositing TDS by due date | 100 percent disallowance of expenditure under Section 40(a)(i) |\n| **Deemed Dividend** | Section 2(22)(e) | Loan received by shareholder holding 10 percent plus voting rights | Taxable as deemed dividend to the extent of accumulated profits |\n\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> **Depreciation Rules:** Assets acquired and put to use for more than 180 days during the financial year qualify for full normal depreciation as prescribed under Income-tax Rules, 1962.\n\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> **Disallowance Traps:** Failure to deposit deducted TDS on payments to non-residents within the statutory due date specified under Section 139(1) results in absolute disallowance of the expense.",
